    pr_ref:  # cdae
    description: "Observed 2026-09-04 while measuring INV-kotob on Alamofire: `open class Session: @unchecked Sendable {` (Source/Core/Session.swift, 1457 lines) is NOT among the extracted symbols — its methods (`webSocketRequest`, `withAllRequests`, `cancelAllRequests`, ...) are extracted as top-level FUNCTIONS instead (82 symbols from the file, no class). A three-line snippet `open class Session: @unchecked Sendable { func go() {} }` extracts `Session` as a class, so the miss is file-specific: something earlier in the file drops the class_declaration (an ERROR node from newer Swift syntax is the first hypothesis; not confirmed). Consequences: every `Session.x` / `session.x` receiver in Alamofire is treated as an EXTERNAL type (`swift:Session:0-0:request:unresolved` reached the module slot in the kotob arm B), and the class's 100+ methods are mis-kinded. Repro: `analyze_swift` on a dir holding only that file; check for a `class` symbol named `Session`. Lab: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/higob_swiftobjc_09032026/README.md (pass 1 read-back)."  # cdae
    fields:  # cdae
      statement: "Every class_declaration in a parsed Swift file MUST yield a class symbol, and its methods MUST be kinded as methods of that class, never as top-level functions."  # cdae
      root_cause: "UNCONFIRMED and file-specific: a three-line snippet with the same header extracts a class; Source/Core/Session.swift (1457 lines) yields 82 symbols and no class. First hypothesis: an ERROR node from newer Swift syntax earlier in the file swallows the class_declaration."  # cdae
